  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read everything back from Kibana to confirm.\n\n1. **Data view 'Kibana Sample Data Flights'** (`d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 31 fields, including `Carrier`, `Cancelled` (boolean), `FlightDelay`, `DestCountry`, `AvgTicketPrice` and `DistanceKilometers`. Its time field is `timestamp`.\n\n2. **Dashboard 'S4-d25cd831 Flights'** (`a7b3835e-e620-4474-9f9e-31104b1fbac8`) has exactly two panels, both on the flights data view:\n   - a bar chart of count over `timestamp` (date histogram)\n   - a horizontal bar chart of count by `Carrier` (terms)\n   \n   I chose the default time range of last 7 days and the bar chart types. The request didn't specify these.\n\n3. **Alert rule 'S4-d25cd831 Cancelled flights'** (`1556114f-2e0c-4531-af69-30c63fedf759`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every 1 minute and fires when the count of documents over the last 5 minutes is greater than 10. The query is `Cancelled : true`. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it. I used the `stackAlerts` consumer, which is the one Stack Management uses.\n\n4. **Carrier panel renamed** to 'Flights by carrier'. The dashboard still has exactly two panels. Reading the rule back shows it enabled, with status `ok` and its last execution at 17:30:42Z.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
